{"name":"Buccaneer Blue","hex":"#035b8d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buccaneer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a group of seamen who cruised on their own account on the Spanish Main and in the Pacific in the 17th century, who were similar to pirates but did not prey on ships of their own nation.","A pirate."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To engage in piracy against any but one's own nation's ships."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buccaneer"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buccaneer","blocks":[{"text":"Buccaneers were privateers and pirates operating in the Caribbean Sea during the 17th and 18th centuries. First established as early as 1625 on northwestern side of Hispaniola after the devastations of Osorio, their heyday was from the Restoration in 1660 until about 1688, during a time when governments in the Caribbean area were not strong enough to suppress them. Martinique was a home port for French buccaneers as well as pirates like Captain Crapeau."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pyle_pirate_handsome.jpg/330px-Pyle_pirate_handsome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buccaneer","wikidata":"Q850220"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Pyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg/500px-Pyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Pyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg/1280px-Pyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Pyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg/500px-Pyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Howard Pyle – Buccaneers attacking a much larger Spanish galleon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APyle_pirates_approaching_ship.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Pyle_pirates_city.jpg/500px-Pyle_pirates_city.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Pyle_pirates_city.jpg/1280px-Pyle_pirates_city.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Pyle_pirates_city.jpg/500px-Pyle_pirates_city.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_